= degrees of belief =
====== short ======
Are or ought credences to be governed by the laws of probability

====== related to ======
??[[confirmation_holism]]
++[[confirmation]]

[[local:zz.texts/lewis1980-subjectivistsGuideObjectiveChance.pdf]]
[[local:zz.texts/kyburg1978-subjectiveProbabilityCriticismsReflectionsProblems.pdf]]

= main =
Fine we don't actually walk around with real valued credences in our beliefs all
the time.

== for ==
=== credences are connected to belief in probabilities ===
    A certain coin is scheduled to be tossed at noon today. You are sure that
    this chosen coin is fair: it has a 50% chance of falling heads and a 50%
    chance of falling tails. You have no other relevant information. Consider
    the proposition that the coin tossed at noon today falls heads. To what
    degree should you now believe that proposition? <br>
    [[local:zz.texts/lewis1980-subjectivistsGuideObjectiveChance.pdf]]
More generally, if one believes that there is a N% probability of E occurring,
then ones confidence in the belief that E will occur simply is (barring
confusion, limit cognitive resources etc.) N%.

Anyone who thinks there is a N% chance of E, but believes that E will occur with
a different credence is either
A) likely to have their expectations falsified (if they are right about the N% chance)
B) unable to reason probabilistically (if their credence generates likely expectations)

Artificial examples with balls in urns or decks of cards, seem to work
beautifully. The anti-subjective probability


=== dutch book ===
Apparently this line of argument is due to frank Ramsey, or at least he used
it.
The traditional argument is that it is “irrational” to hold beliefs with
credences that allow a dutch book to be made against them. But the Dutch book is
just a way of codifying that things do not go as the subject expects. They make
each bet (supposedly) in the expectation of making a profit. In a dutch book we
can demonstrate that those expectations are not fulfilled. Thus the appeal of
having probabilities drop out altogether. But the point is really that one's
expectations are likely to (or if there is a dutch book, are certain to) be
mistaken.

== against ==
=== betting odds affected by factors other than confidence ===
value of risk/risk avoidance, doubt concerning payoff
(insurance is a bet in which the expected return is negative but the risk is
considerably lowered)

=== not empirically supported ===
probability theory makes poor predictions about human behaviour. But we expect
people to use heuristics

betting acceptance is not a pure measure of credence. There are other factors
such as risk aversion which may affect the bets one is willing to make.

One can fail to observe the laws of probability in ways that don't lead to a
dutch book. e.g. being more confident (willing to bet on) $A ∧ B$ than $A$.
Um... so? These are other ways of forming credences that are likely to lead you
wrong but unlike the Dutch books they don't guarantee having unexpected
outcomes.



=== vagueness ===
:my_thesis: [[confirmation_holism]]

It is so arbitrary to put numbers on our degrees of belief - how likely is there
to be a red car on the street when I next walk along it?  The numbers feel
palpably arbitrary. I don't seem able to distinguish between believing such a
claim with 70% confidence and with 72% confidence (let alone 72.5). I think
things are pretty likely, very likely, near certain (perhaps beyond reasonable
doubt? So certain that I need not bother checking further before acting?) or
absolutely certain. But why no numbers?

Because we dont really model things we consider to be very unlikely. We dont
waste the mental/neurological resources to model unlikely events. So say we
actively model (develop expectations for) a range of expectations that we are
confident will occur.

